# english-places.kb - English / Places
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 6 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

city | definition | A city is a large settlement, and the word is also the lowest formal rank in a hierarchy running city, town, village and hamlet. No fixed population turns a place into a city, so two countries apply the same word to settlements of wildly different size. The legal designation inside a given country is what actually counts, and it usually reflects administration rather than how many people live there. | city, town, village, settlement, population, rank | what is a city, difference between city and town, how big is a city | city | syudad
country | definition | A country is a state with its own government and territory, and the word stretches well past independent nations, since constituent countries such as England and Scotland conduct no foreign policy. This is where the everyday and the political meanings come apart cleanly. In ordinary speech a country is a landscape and a culture, and in law it is sovereignty, and both usages are correct inside their own context. | country, state, government, territory, sovereignty, nation | what is a country, is england a country, country vs nation | country | bansa
capital | definition | A capital is the city where a government sits, and the word also carries the sense of an upper limit, since both ideas share being first. Not every capital is the largest city, which is the interesting part, because Ottawa and Buenos Aires are both capitals while the biggest city in each country is somewhere else entirely. The word is worth flagging because questions about capitals are common and the answer is not guessable from population. | capital, government, city, largest, limit, seat | what is a capital city, is the capital always the largest, ottawa population | capital | kabangalan
region | definition | A region is an area defined by something other than a national border, whether geography, climate, history or an administrative body, and that loose sense is why people disagree about how many regions a place has. A region can cross a border, and it can be smaller than a province, so the term tends to be avoided in law and used in conversation. Where a fixed division is meant, the administrative vocabulary is the one to reach for. | region, area, border, climate, province, division | what is a region, is a region the same as a province, can a region cross a border | region | rehiyon
neighbourhood | definition | A neighbourhood is part of a town or city, and it is defined less by an official boundary than by shared custom, so two maps can disagree about exactly where it begins. The word carries a social sense as well, since people use it for a community rather than a polygon, and that sense outlives the buildings when residents are displaced. British and American usage both keep the word, which is unusual for such a local concept. | neighbourhood, community, boundary, local, area, custom | what is a neighbourhood, is neighbourhood british, difference between neighbourhood and district | neighbourhood | kapitbahayan
landmark | definition | A landmark is a feature that is easy to recognise and therefore used to say where something is, so both natural and built senses count, as in a mountain landmark and a navigation landmark. The word also implies staying put, which is the part that does not hold, because buildings come down and hills get quarried away. Places stay recognisable mainly because people go on calling them that, not because the structure is still standing. | landmark, recognise, navigation, building, feature, permanent | what is a landmark, natural vs man made landmark, is a landmark permanent | landmark | tandaan
